course: medicine
lesson: 6
title: Burns, sunburn and stings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A burn is skin damaged by heat; red skin means the top layer is hurt, and blisters mean deeper damage.
explain: The rule is cool running water for 20 minutes, never butter, toothpaste or ice: butter holds the heat in and ice hurts the skin more.
explain: Sunburn is a burn too, from invisible ultraviolet rays, and you can get it on a cloudy day; a tan is not healthy skin, it is damage.
explain: A honeybee leaves its barbed sting in your skin, so scrape it out sideways quickly; a wasp keeps its sting and can sting again.
explain: For a few people a sting causes anaphylaxis, a whole-body allergic reaction: a swollen face or trouble breathing means call 999 at once.
fact: Burns are cooled under cool running water for 20 minutes, then covered loosely with cling film or a clean dressing.
fact: Butter, toothpaste or ice should never be put on a burn.
fact: A blister protects damaged skin while new skin grows, so it is best left alone; popping it can let in germs.
fact: Burns bigger than the child's palm, deep burns, or burns on the face, hands or feet need medical help.
fact: A hot drink can badly scald a small child's thin skin even 15 minutes after it was made; any scald on a small child needs 999 or A&E.
fact: Ultraviolet rays damage skin cells, and repeated sunburn in childhood raises the risk of skin cancer later in life.
fact: You can burn on cloudy days and in the shade near water or sand.
fact: A honeybee's barbed sting stays in the skin and is scraped out sideways with a fingernail or card as soon as possible, because its venom sac keeps pumping while it stays in.
fact: Swelling of the face or tongue, wheezing or fainting after a sting is anaphylaxis and an emergency: call 999 and use an adrenaline pen, a pen-shaped injection, if there is one.
fact: Dock leaves have no proven medicine in them; washing with soap and water and a cool cloth helps nettle stings.
fact: Jellyfish stings are rinsed with seawater, tentacles removed with tweezers or gloves, and hot water or a hot pack eases the pain; weeing on them does not help and can make some stings worse.
fact: Sparklers burn at over a thousand degrees, many times hotter than a cooking oven, so they are only for older children, held with gloves, and used ones go into a bucket of water.
fact: Most insect bites and stings are not dangerous, even abroad, but a doctor or pharmacist can check one that swells up.
exercise: What is the first thing to do for a burn? | Cool it under cool running water for 20 minutes | Rub butter on it to keep the air off the sore skin | Put ice cubes straight on it for an hour | Cover it with toothpaste to cool it down
exercise: Why should you not put butter on a burn? | It keeps the heat in the skin | It is too greasy to wash off afterwards | It makes the burn itch for days | It only feeds the germs and nothing else
exercise: You've got a blister from a burn. Should you pop it? | No, it protects the skin while new skin grows | Yes, pop it at once to let the heat out | Yes, with a pin from the sewing box | No, but squeeze it hard so it goes flat
exercise: Which burn needs medical help? | One bigger than the child's palm | A tiny red mark that soon fades away | A small burn that was cooled well | Any burn on the elbow, however small
exercise: Your little sister pulled hot tea on herself. What should happen? | Cool it for 20 minutes and call 999 or go to A&E | Wrap her up tight in a blanket and wait | Rub some cream on it and see how it goes | Put her straight in a cold bath full of ice cubes
exercise: What causes sunburn? | Ultraviolet rays damaging skin cells | Heat coming up from the hot sand | Salt from the sea drying on the skin | Sweating too much in the hot sun
exercise: Can you get sunburnt on a cloudy day? | Yes, UV rays get through clouds | No, clouds block all the rays that burn | Only if you stay out at night | Only in winter when it's cold
exercise: A bee sting is still in your skin. What should you do? | Scrape it out sideways quickly with a nail or card | Leave it in for a day so it falls out | Squeeze it out slowly with your fingers | Push it deeper so the poison spreads out thin
exercise: Why does a bee leave its sting but a wasp doesn't? | The bee's sting has barbs | Wasps have no sting at all, they bite | Bees are angrier than wasps | Wasps eat their stings afterwards
exercise: A friend's face swells and he can't breathe after a sting. What is this? | Anaphylaxis, an emergency: call 999 | A normal sting reaction that will pass by itself | Just being dramatic for attention | Hay fever from the flowers nearby
exercise: Do dock leaves cure nettle stings? | No, they have no proven medicine in them | Yes, they contain a cure that kills the sting | Yes, but only the big ones | No, they make stings worse every time
exercise: What helps a jellyfish sting? | Rinsing with seawater and using hot water for the pain | Weeing on it straight away | Rubbing dry sand into it hard | Rinsing it well with a cold fizzy drink from the beach shop
exercise: Why are sparklers dangerous? | They burn at over a thousand degrees | They are full of poison that you breathe | They explode like bombs at the end | They are too heavy for small hands
exercise: You touched a hot radiator and it left a red mark. What should you do? | Cool it under running water | Ignore it, small burns need nothing at all | Put ice straight on it | Rub it hard to warm it back up
exercise: Why does repeated childhood sunburn matter? | It raises the risk of skin cancer later | It makes your bones grow taller | It only causes a few extra freckles, nothing more | It has no effect at all once it peels
exercise: How should a burn be covered after cooling? | Loosely with cling film or a clean dressing | Tightly with a soft fluffy towel | With butter and kitchen foil wrapped round | It should never be covered at all
exercise: Abroad, an ant sting swells up. Who decides what treatment it needs? | A doctor or pharmacist | Nobody, all foreign stings are deadly | Nobody, stings never need care | The cook at the hotel
test: burnt me hand on the oven, nan says stick toothpaste on it. smart? | No, cool it under running water for 20 minutes | Yes, toothpaste cools burns down quick | Yes, minty is best for burns | No, use butter instead, that's what works
test: Dad rubbed a dock leaf on my nettle sting and it felt better. Why might it? | Something cool and wet can soothe and distract | Dock leaves contain a proven cure for stings | The leaf sucks the sting out of the skin | It is magic and nobody knows why
test: In a film, a man pees on a jellyfish sting to cure it. What's the truth? | It does not help and can make some stings worse | It is the best cure, every lifeguard says so | It works but only in the sea | It works if done quickly enough
test: My mate says getting a tan means healthy skin. Right? | No, a tan is a sign of skin damage | Yes, a tan protects you fully from burning | Yes, brown skin can't burn | No, a tan comes from the food you eat
test: Why is a cup of tea dangerous for a toddler even if it's not boiling? | It can still scald thin skin badly | Tea is poisonous to toddlers if they drink it | It makes them hyperactive and they fall | It isn't, tea that has stopped boiling is always safe
test: Your friend was stung, his lips are swelling and he's wheezing. What would a good grown-up do? | Call 999 and use his adrenaline pen | Give him a biscuit and wait to see what happens | Tell him to stop being silly | Put a dock leaf on it and keep him calm
test: Why scrape a bee sting out quickly instead of waiting? | The venom sac keeps pumping while it stays | It grows longer and deeper each minute | It will fly away by itself | The bee comes back for it and stings again
test: Which of these is the best way to have sparklers at a party? | Only for older children, with gloves, and a bucket of water for the used ones | Anyone can hold one bare-handed if they hold it at the very end | Throw the used ones in the bin straight away | Toddlers can hold them if they are careful
test: Ant sting abroad, got some cream at A&E. Are foreign bites always dangerous? | No, most are not, but some need checking | Yes, all foreign bites are deadly | Yes, you must fly home that same day | No, foreign bites never hurt or swell at all
test: What would happen if you put ice straight on a burn? | It could damage the skin more | It would heal the burn in a minute | It would make the blister vanish | Nothing at all, ice is just very cold water
test: Which day can you get sunburnt? | Even a cloudy day at the beach | Only on a hot clear day in summer | Never in England, it's too far north | Only in the afternoon
exam: Identify the recommended initial treatment of a minor thermal burn. | Cooling beneath cool running water for twenty minutes | Application of butter or other fats to retain moisture | Application of ice directly to the skin | Application of toothpaste to the affected area
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises sunburn? | Damage to skin cells by ultraviolet radiation, possible beneath cloud | Damage occurring exclusively on cloudless days of high temperature | A harmless darkening of the skin | Damage caused solely by heat reflected from sand
exam: It is given that a child's face and tongue have swollen and breathing has become laboured following an insect sting. State the appropriate course of action. | Emergency services are to be summoned and adrenaline administered if available | A dock leaf is to be applied to the site of the sting | The child is to be left to recover without assistance | The sting is to be squeezed out slowly and the child observed for an hour
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning blisters arising from a burn. | They are best left intact, as they protect the healing skin | They ought to be punctured at once to release the heat | They indicate that the burn is wholly superficial and harmless | They are to be covered with butter
